      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Chewelah was officially incorporated on January 26, 1903, the same year its newspaper The Independent began publishing.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chewelah%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1879 the post office was reestablished with Major John Simms, an Indian Agent, as postmaster, marking a key moment in the town’s early communication network.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chewelah%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Chewelah’s first school opened in 1869, making it one of the earliest educational centers in the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chewelah%2C_Washington",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Chewelah sits in the heart of the Colville River Valley, surrounded by forests, rivers, and the Colville National Forest, offering abundant outdoor adventures.",
